#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""acp-dispatch — one consistent way to hand a slice to an agent and get its report back.

    acp-dispatch <agent> --model M --name NAME (-p PROMPT | -f BRIEF) [-C DIR]
                 (--model may be omitted for zcode only, which has just two models)
                 [--effort LEVEL] [--context SIZE]
                 [--task TASK | --out DIR] [--perm all|deny] [--timeout S]
                 [--lead NAME] [--detach [--no-wait] [--wait-timeout S]] [--dry-run] [--json]
                 [--min-headroom PCT] [--est-pct PCT] [--checkpoint] [--no-preflight]
                 [--no-auto-resume]
    acp-dispatch footer --report PATH [--lead NAME]      the shared footer, filled in, for an
                                                         Agent-tool prompt (paste it verbatim)
    acp-dispatch check (PATH)...                         classify reports: PATH is a report file,
                                                         a jobs/<name>.json record, or an out dir
    acp-dispatch finalize RECORD --exit RC               (internal) what the detached runner calls
    acp-dispatch extend RECORD SECONDS                  add seconds, or 0 for no deadline
    acp-dispatch resume RECORD [--timeout S] [-p TEXT | -f FILE] [--detach --no-wait]
                 [--no-auto-resume]
    acp-dispatch reroute RECORD --agent A --model M [--detach --no-wait]
    acp-dispatch auto-resume RECORD --attempt N          (internal) what the scheduled resume
                                                         runner calls after a quota stop
    acp-dispatch reap [RECORD|DIR]... [--quiet]          finalize every unfinished record whose
                                                         process is gone, and re-submit a
                                                         scheduled auto-resume a reboot removed
                                                         (idempotent, any time)
    acp-dispatch reap --install | --uninstall            the launchd sweep (RunAtLoad + every 30 min)

What it replaces: the dispatch recipe that bigteam, model-routing and home-agents.md used to
spell out in prose and every dispatcher retyped — the report-file naming, "write the full
report there, then reply with only a pointer", the `.done` marker, the jobs record, the bounded
`until [ -s ]` waiter, `--model`/`--perm`/`--timeout`, and (since 2026-10-08) the 4,000-character
cap on a sub-agent's final message and the never-end-on-a-question rule. The rules live once, in
docs/dispatch-footer.md, and are appended to every brief.

Contract (the same for ACP agents run here and for Claude Code Agent-tool sub-agents, which
get the footer pasted into their prompt because the Agent tool cannot be driven from a script):
    report   $OUT/<name>-report.md     the deliverable; first line `STATUS: working` or
                                        `BLOCKED: <question>`; last line `DONE`
    done     <report>.done              touched last; waiters watch this, never the report
    record   $OUT/jobs/<name>.json      who launched it, every path, the exact waiter to re-arm
    $OUT     ~/.local/state/bigteam/<task>/ (default) or --out; never the session scratchpad,
             which is wiped when the session restarts (2026-10-05)

--model is checked before anything is written or detached (2026-10-10: a bad agy model used to
detach, fail inside the launchd job and surface only as `no-report` from the waiter). The
agent's model list comes from `acp-run <agent> --info` (its ACP session/new list, which is what
acp-run itself matches against), cached per agent for 6 h under ~/.cache/acp-dispatch and
re-read once on a miss; a miss exits 2 with the `have:` list. If the probe itself fails the
dispatch goes ahead with a warning. --no-model-check skips it.

--effort LEVEL and --context SIZE are acp-run's normalized flags (acp-run --help), passed to it
first-class after --model and recorded in the job record. They are checked together with the model,
before anything is written or detached, by `acp-run <agent> --info --model M --effort E --context C`
(it applies them to a fresh session without a prompt): an agent or model that cannot take the value
exits 2 here with acp-run's list of valid values. --acp-arg still passes anything else through.

When the agent stops without a DONE or BLOCKED report and acp-run failed (a quota or plan
refusal, a crash, a timeout), finalize appends a last line `NO-REPORT: <class> (<agent>,
<model>[, retry at <time>]) ...` to the report, so the report itself says it is not a
deliverable, and the slice classifies as no-report (exit 3). The `rearm` waiter runs
`acp-dispatch check <record>` once `.done` appears, so it exits non-zero (1 partial,
3 no-report, 4 BLOCKED) and prints the verdict instead of exiting 0 on any finish.

For an ACP agent the final message (acp-run's stdout) is saved to $OUT/<name>-final.txt. If
the agent did not write the report file but its final message carries the content, the report
is recovered from it and the record says so (`report_source: final-message`) — stdout from
acp-run is never truncated, so nothing is lost; the warning tells you the brief was not followed.
Not when acp-run itself failed that turn (`FAILED <class>` on its stderr or a non-zero exit): the
final message is then kept above a `NO-REPORT:` line and never gets a DONE (`report_source:
final-message-failed`). A model refusal is such a failure: acp-run classifies a short reply that
starts "Sorry, I cannot ..." / "I must decline ..." as `FAILED refused` (2026-10-10: agy
gemini-pro-agent declined a security slice, the refusal was recovered, given DONE and checked
`done`), and the note reads `NO-REPORT: refused (<agent>, <model>): <first sentence> ...`.

Time budget (2026-10-10, wedge-grants/stlib): the brief copy ends with a "Time budget" section that
gives the agent its --timeout in minutes and tells it to write the report before a commit, a push or
any long command. acp-run gets --wrapup-grace (default 300 s, 0 turns it off) and a wrap-up prompt
naming the report: when the timeout cancels the turn, the agent gets one last turn to write the report
with first line `STATUS: timed out` and last line DONE. Such a report classifies `done` with
`acp-run exit=124 (timed out ...)`, and dispatch and check exit 1, never 0. The rearm waiter allows
timeout + wrap-up grace + 300 s.

A quota stop is resumed in place, automatically (operator 2026-10-18: "tokens spent thinking about
a problem are something"; three codex slices had been stopped by the 5-hour limit and the lead was
about to re-task them from fresh briefs). When finalize writes `NO-REPORT: quota (...)` and the
record has an ACP session id and the agent advertises session/load or session/resume, finalize
schedules a one-shot `acp-dispatch auto-resume RECORD` under launchd (the same mechanism as
--detach, so it outlives the lead's session) for the reset time plus a 5-minute margin: the reset
time comes from acp-run's stderr, else from the pool's `resets_at` in aiuse's snapshot, else a
30-minute back-off. Before resuming, the runner gates on the pool's live headroom
(`aiuse --available --json --live`, 20% left; re-checked up to 6 times, 10 min apart, then it gives
up with the manual command in the record). The resume reopens the same session with a prompt
saying the stop was the usage limit, not the agent: continue, redo nothing finished, complete the
report and the `.done`. Three automatic attempts per slice, then `given-up`. The record's
`auto_resume` field carries state, attempt, `not_before_iso`, label and runner; `resume_count`
links the runs. `--no-auto-resume` (dispatch and resume) turns it off for that slice;
ACP_DISPATCH_NO_AUTO_RESUME=1 for a whole session. A record that already has `closed` is never
scheduled. The `rearm` waiter does not end on such a stop: `wait` sees the pending auto-resume and
keeps waiting through it to the real end (bounded: reset + gate window + 15 min), so a lead's
background waiter needs no change. `check` on a record in that state prints `resume-scheduled` and
exits 1 (still running, like `running`/`pending`); exit 3 keeps meaning a real no-report. A quota
stop that cannot be resumed automatically (no session id, agent without load/resume, opted out,
attempts exhausted) stays exit 3 and the `check` line ends with the exact
`acp-dispatch resume <record>` command and the retry time.

A detached slice that dies without running finalize (a reboot's SIGTERM, SIGKILL, OOM, power
loss, launchd unloading it) used to leave `exit: null, finished: null` forever, so every reader
showed it running (2026-10-17: trackpad-settings, pid 51407, killed by a reboot, listed as running
hours later; a rearm waiter on it sat until its timeout). Since 2026-10-18: (1) the detached
runner traps TERM/HUP/INT, forwards TERM to acp-run (which cancels the turn and stops the agent),
SIGKILLs it after 25 s, and finalizes with 128+signal (`NO-REPORT: killed: SIGTERM (...)`); it also
writes `<name>.exit` before finalize. (2) The record stores `pid_start` (`ps -o lstart=`) with the
pid, so one shared rule, `dead_reason()`, says whether an unfinished record's process is gone: pid
not alive, or alive but not the same process (a reused pid after a reboot); a record from before
`pid_start` counts as running only while the command line still looks like this slice. (3)
`acp-dispatch reap` finalizes such a record once, under its lock: exit from `<name>.exit`, else
launchd's last exit status for the record's label (`launchctl print gui/$UID/<label>`), else 255
(`lost`); `finished` is the last write to the log/report/stderr; the record carries `reaped:
{reason, exit_source, at}`. A record whose report ends in DONE with `.done` present is finished
normally (the runner died after the agent delivered). `check`, `wait` and `resume` apply the rule
first, so a dead slice is never "running" to a reader; `wait` returns at once on one. The sweep
`acp-dispatch reap ~/.local/state/bigteam` runs from launchd (`reap --install`;
launchd/com.djbclark.acp-dispatch-reap.plist) at login and every 30 min. The reaped slice's
stderr still decides the class: a quota refusal there follows the quota path (auto-resume).

A scheduled auto-resume survives a reboot (2026-10-18): `launchctl submit` jobs are removed by a
reboot or logout, leaving a record that says `scheduled` with nothing to run it. The same sweep
(`reap`, so at login and every 30 min) also finds each finished, unclosed record whose
`auto_resume.state` is pending (scheduled, waiting-headroom, resuming) and whose launchd label is
no longer loaded (`launchctl print` says so; an unavailable launchctl changes nothing) and submits
the same attempt again: same label and `not_before` (already past: the runner starts at once, still
gated on pool headroom). Under the record's lock, so concurrent sweeps submit once; a closed,
manually resumed, rerouted, given-up or newer-attempt record (its state is no longer pending, or the
label is a newer one) is never revived. `auto_resume.resubmitted` lists the epochs; a refused submit is kept in
`auto_resume.resubmit_failed` and retried by the next sweep. `reap` prints
`resubmitted<TAB>name<TAB>record<TAB>label attempt N (...)` per re-submission (or `resubmit-failed`).

`wait` returns only once the record is finalized (or reaped), not when `.done` appears: the agent
itself touches `.done` as its last act, so acp-run and finalize may still be running (2026-10-18:
`wait && resume` hit "slice process is still running"). `resume` also waits up to 120 s
(ACP_DISPATCH_FINALIZE_WAIT) for finalize when `.done` exists. finalize records the model acp-run
actually applied (`model_applied`, from the log's `applied` row) and `resume` passes that, so a
family alias such as `fable` that a resumed session's model list resolves differently cannot kill
the resume (2026-10-18: "no model 'fable' (ambiguous: claude-fable-5-1, claude-fable-5)").

Exit codes (dispatch and check):
    0    report delivered and ends in DONE (dispatch: and acp-run exited 0)
    1    report present but unfinished (no DONE), or delivered with a non-zero acp-run exit;
         also `running`, `pending` and `resume-scheduled` (a quota stop whose in-place resume is
         scheduled: still running, wait for the rearm waiter)
    2    usage (missing or unknown --model, bad name, report basename Claude Code would refuse, ...)
    3    delivery failure: the agent finished (or died) and there is no report, or its report
         ends in a NO-REPORT: line (acp-run failed before the agent wrote DONE) and no automatic
         resume is pending; for a quota stop the line carries the manual resume command
    4    BLOCKED: the report's first line is a question for the lead; answer it, then resume
    5    pool unavailable before launch; re-plan with --exclude (no slice attempt consumed)
    124  timed out waiting for the .done marker (--detach without --no-wait)
    (a reaped record's acp-run exit is 255 `lost` when neither an exit file nor launchd knows it;
     a signalled runner's is 128+signal, e.g. 143 for SIGTERM)
Exit 0 is still not success: verify the outcome (diff, tests, the report's content).
"""
